import{_ as a,c as t,o as n,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.ARG558ZT.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"DynamoDB and RDS: choose persistence by access pattern","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"aws/databases.md","filePath":"aws/databases.md"}'),o={name:"aws/databases.md"};function r(i,e,d,c,l,p){return n(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="dynamodb-and-rds-choose-persistence-by-access-pattern" tabindex="-1">DynamoDB and RDS: choose persistence by access pattern <a class="header-anchor" href="#dynamodb-and-rds-choose-persistence-by-access-pattern" aria-label="Permalink to “DynamoDB and RDS: choose persistence by access pattern”">​</a></h1><p>A database decision starts with the data and operations: what must be read together, which changes must be atomic, and how the application will find its records. “NoSQL scales” and “SQL has tables” are not enough to choose a design.</p><h2 id="the-relational-foundation" tabindex="-1">The relational foundation <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-relational-foundation" aria-label="Permalink to “The relational foundation”">​</a></h2><p>A relational database organizes rows into tables with defined columns and constraints. Primary keys identify rows; foreign keys describe valid references. A primary key need not be generated automatically. Joins combine related records; aggregates summarize groups. Indexes can accelerate selected queries while adding storage and write work.</p><p>Transactions group related changes with defined isolation behavior. For an order, inserting the order header and its items in one transaction prevents a partially saved order. Validate business rules too: a database cannot infer whether a particular customer is allowed to change that order.</p><p>Use exact decimal values or integer minor units for money with an explicit currency and rounding policy. A floating-point <code>price</code> from an introductory ORM example is not a general financial data model.</p><h2 id="dynamodb-s-key-based-model" tabindex="-1">DynamoDB&#39;s key-based model <a class="header-anchor" href="#dynamodb-s-key-based-model" aria-label="Permalink to “DynamoDB&#39;s key-based model”">​</a></h2><p>A DynamoDB table has a partition key and optionally a sort key. Together they identify an item. Non-key attributes can vary between items, but flexible attributes do not remove the need for application validation and a deliberate model.</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>PK            SK              title          stock</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>PRODUCT#42    DETAILS         Tea kettle</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>PRODUCT#42    STOCK                          12</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>PRODUCT#57    DETAILS         Coffee grinder</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is a conceptual single-table example, not a required schema. Separate product and stock tables, as in the course, are another choice. Start with the queries you need, then choose keys and indexes.</p><p><code>GetItem</code> uses the full primary key. <code>Query</code> selects one partition-key value with optional sort-key conditions. <code>Scan</code> examines the table or index broadly. A filter expression is not a substitute for an efficient key condition: filtering happens after items are read.</p><h2 id="indexes-and-consistency" tabindex="-1">Indexes and consistency <a class="header-anchor" href="#indexes-and-consistency" aria-label="Permalink to “Indexes and consistency”">​</a></h2><p>An LSI keeps the table&#39;s partition key and offers another sort key; it is defined at table creation. A GSI can use a different partition and sort key. Projecting selected attributes can avoid some extra reads, but indexes cost storage and write maintenance.</p><p>Table and LSI reads can request strong consistency; GSI reads are eventually consistent. A read through a GSI may briefly lag a successful write. Choose the read path to match the user-visible promise. <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadConsistency.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DynamoDB consistency</a>.</p><p>Use conditional writes for statements such as “create only if absent” or “decrement only if sufficient stock remains.” Reading a value and later overwriting it without a condition can lose concurrent changes. Transactions can coordinate supported operations across items and tables. Retrying a batch API requires handling its reported unprocessed items, not assuming every submitted operation succeeded.</p><p>DynamoDB Streams exposes item changes for downstream processing. Stream consumers still need duplicate-safe effects and a recovery strategy. A stream is not an application database query endpoint.</p><h2 id="rds-and-managed-sql" tabindex="-1">RDS and managed SQL <a class="header-anchor" href="#rds-and-managed-sql" aria-label="Permalink to “RDS and managed SQL”">​</a></h2><p>RDS manages supported relational database engines and infrastructure tasks. You still own schemas, queries, indexes, access, migrations and application behavior. PostgreSQL and MySQL differ; pick one engine and use its actual driver and types.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Primary purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Automated backups / point-in-time recovery</td><td>Restore data to a supported recovery point</td></tr><tr><td>Snapshot</td><td>Preserve a database state for later restoration</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-AZ deployment</td><td>Improve availability and failover characteristics</td></tr><tr><td>Read replica</td><td>Serve suitable read workloads and reduce primary read load</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The exact Multi-AZ and replica behavior depends on deployment type. A standby in a traditional Multi-AZ DB instance deployment is not simply an extra read endpoint. Replication is also not a replacement for recovery from an accidental valid delete. <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RDS deployment documentation</a>.</p><h2 id="lambda-nestjs-and-orm-connections" tabindex="-1">Lambda, NestJS and ORM connections <a class="header-anchor" href="#lambda-nestjs-and-orm-connections" aria-label="Permalink to “Lambda, NestJS and ORM connections”">​</a></h2><p>A NestJS adapter can initialize the application once per reusable environment and forward events through a serverless HTTP adapter. That makes an existing HTTP application runnable in Lambda; it does not remove startup time, connection limits or framework overhead.</p><p>ORM configuration needs the engine, endpoint, credentials, database and compatible TLS settings. Keep credentials in an approved secret store. Use reviewed migrations rather than automatic schema synchronization for production.</p><p>Each concurrent Lambda environment can create its own connection pool. A pool size that looks small locally can multiply into hundreds of connections. Bound concurrency and pooling, and consider RDS Proxy where appropriate. Database transactions must always use the intended connection/session.</p><p>For TLS failures, diagnose trust configuration and the actual error. Use the correct RDS CA bundle and server verification. <code>rejectUnauthorized: false</code> removes verification and is not the default solution to connection problems. A <code>pg_hba.conf</code>-style error does not uniquely diagnose one certificate issue. <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/UsingWithRDS.SSL.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RDS TLS guidance</a>.</p><h2 id="broader-database-awareness" tabindex="-1">Broader database awareness <a class="header-anchor" href="#broader-database-awareness" aria-label="Permalink to “Broader database awareness”">​</a></h2><p>ElastiCache supports low-latency caching; DocumentDB targets document workloads; Neptune targets graphs; Redshift targets analytical warehousing; Athena queries data such as files in S3. These are awareness topics in the supplied slides, not evidence that every engine has been mastered. Existing <a href="./../node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.html">PostgreSQL notes</a> provide deeper SQL application context.</p><p>Next: <a href="./file-imports.html">file imports</a>.</p>`,28)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
